WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
The leak prevention roadmap starts with explicit ownership and cleanup.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:09.400
Every resource should have an owner, a lifetime, and a release path.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.220
Borrowed access should remain temporary.

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.400
Transfer and sharing should be visible in the API and type design.

5
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.680
Prefer RAII, values, containers, and smart pointers.

6
00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.980
Values and containers reduce manual allocation.

7
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:27.280
RAII makes cleanup follow object lifetime.

8
00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:30.180
unique_ptr expresses exclusive ownership.

9
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:37.560
shared_ptr should represent real shared ownership, and weak_ptr should be used when observation should not extend lifetime.

10
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:41.280
Break cycles and limit unintended retention.

11
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:49.380
Review parent-child structures, callbacks, observer lists, event systems, caches, queues, and registries.

12
00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:57.560
Define eviction, expiration, deregistration, backpressure, and shutdown behavior before retained state grows without a boundary.

13
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.160
Wrap custom resources in types that match their cleanup contracts.

14
00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.180
Document allocator, pool, and arena rules.

15
00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:13.320
Specialized lifecycle strategies can be valuable, but only when callers understand when resources are released and when objects become invalid.

16
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:19.360
Finally, test, monitor, review, and revisit resource lifecycles.

17
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:25.940
Use leak detection, lifecycle tests, shutdown tests, diagnostics, and CI checks.

18
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.080
Treat ownership changes as carefully as logic changes.

19
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:33.820
Leak prevention is a lifecycle discipline, not a final cleanup pass.

20
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:41.940
When a team treats lifecycle design as normal engineering practice, leaks become easier to reason about and easier to prevent.

21
00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:47.800
The result is C++ software that stays predictable under real operating conditions.
